; T2.4 (2026-09 revision): Two independent 32-bit subchannels at BL16 = 64 B
; per access (JESD79-5); capacity 65536 x 64 x 64 B x 32 x 1 x 2 = 16 GiB.
; Derived from the live simulators/nvmain/Config/DDR5_4800_DRAM.config with
; BusWidth 32, tCCD 8, RANKS 1, COLS 64 (see access_granularity_study.md §5).
; Device count: NVMain derives devices per rank as BusWidth / DeviceWidth
; (Ranks/StandardRank/StandardRank.cpp:125), so BusWidth 32 with DeviceWidth 8 gives
; 4 devices per subchannel, 8 on the DIMM (one rank of x8 16 Gb parts). The per-device
; EIDD currents below are therefore NOT rescaled by hand; do not halve them again.
;
; ============================================================================
; F4 (2026-09-21): WRITE-PATH AND POWER-DOWN TIMINGS BROUGHT TO JEDEC DDR5-4800
; ============================================================================
; Before this change tCAS/tRCD/tRP/tRAS/tRFC/tREFW were DDR5-4800 values but
; tCWD/tWTR/tWR/tRTP/tPD/tXP/tRDPDEN/tWRPDEN/tWRAPDEN were still the cycle
; counts of NVMain's DDR3-1333 template, running at CLK 2400. That made every
; DDR5 write about 5x too cheap in write latency and 7x too cheap in write
; recovery, which flattered the DDR5 baseline, above all in the write-dominated
; AlexNet OFMAP burst.
;
; Clock: CLK 2400 -> tCK = 1 / 2400 MHz = 0.4166667 ns. JESD79-5 Table 521
; gives tCK(avg) min 0.416 ns for DDR5-4800, so these agree to 0.7 ps.
; Where JEDEC writes max(n nCK, t ns) the arithmetic below is done at
; tCK = 0.4166667 ns and the larger term wins.
;
;   key        old -> new    new in ns   JEDEC source and arithmetic
;   tCWD         7 -> 38      15.833 ns  JESD79-5 Table 471 (DDR5-4800 speed
;                                        bins, standard p.354): DDR5-4800B
;                                        CWL = CL - 2 = 38 nCK. 38 x 0.4166667
;                                        = 15.833 ns. Micron [29] Die Rev A
;                                        Table 1 confirms the 4800B bin at
;                                        CL 40 / tCK 0.416 ns.
;   tWTR         5 -> 6        2.500 ns  JESD79-5 Table 521 (standard p.454):
;                                        tWTR_S = max(4 nCK, 2.5 ns).
;                                        2.5 / 0.4166667 = 6.0 nCK,
;                                        max(4, 6) = 6. See the mapping note
;                                        beside tWTR below for why _S and not
;                                        _L (= max(16 nCK, 10 ns) = 24).
;   tWR         10 -> 72      30.000 ns  JESD79-5 Table 521: tWR = 29.952 ns in
;                                        the DDR5-4800 column, which is the
;                                        30.000 ns nominal scaled and rounded
;                                        down to 1 ps, i.e. exactly
;                                        72 x 0.416 ns. 30 / 0.4166667 = 72.0.
;   tRTP         5 -> 18       7.500 ns  JESD79-5 Table 521:
;                                        tRTP = max(12 nCK, 7.5 ns).
;                                        7.5 / 0.4166667 = 18.0 nCK,
;                                        max(12, 18) = 18.
;   tPD          6 -> 18       7.500 ns  JESD79-5 Table 272 (power-down timing
;                                        parameters, standard p.144):
;                                        tPD = max(7.5 ns, 8 nCK) -> max(18, 8)
;                                        = 18.
;   tXP          6 -> 18       7.500 ns  JESD79-5 Table 272:
;                                        tXP = max(7.5 ns, 8 nCK) = 18.
;   tRDPDEN     24 -> 49       20.417 ns JESD79-5 Table 272:
;                                        tRDPDEN = RL + RBL/2 + 1 nCK.
;                                        RL = tAL + tCAS = 0 + 40; BL16 on this
;                                        32-bit subchannel is tBURST 8 nCK, so
;                                        RBL/2 = 8. 40 + 8 + 1 = 49.
;   tWRPDEN     19 -> 119      49.583 ns JESD79-5 Table 272:
;                                        tWRPDEN = WL + WBL/2 + ceil(tWR/tCK)
;                                        + 1 = 38 + 8 + 72 + 1 = 119.
;   (F4b keys are listed in the second block below.)
;   tWRAPDEN    22 -> 119      49.583 ns JESD79-5 Table 272:
;                                        tWRAPDEN = WL + WBL/2 + WR + 1, WR
;                                        being the MR6-programmed write
;                                        recovery in nCK = 72.
;                                        38 + 8 + 72 + 1 = 119. (NVMain parses
;                                        this key but no timing code reads it,
;                                        so it is inert; corrected so the file
;                                        does not carry a DDR3 number.)
;
; NOT changed, and why (see the F4 report for the full argument):
;   tXPDLL 17  DDR5 has no DLL-off slow-exit power-down (JESD79-5 Table 271:
;              DLL On, Fast exit, for both active and precharged power-down),
;              so no JEDEC parameter maps onto it. NVMain only reads it in the
;              STANDARDRANK_PDPS branch, which PowerDownMode FASTEXIT never
;              reaches, so it is unreachable as well as unsourceable.
;   tXS 5, tXSDLL 512  self-refresh exit. NVMain never enters self refresh;
;              the only reader is the DRAMPower2 trace writer, and its
;              AddHook PostTrace line is commented out at the bottom of this
;              file. Inert.
;   tOST 1, tRTRS 1  ODT switching and rank-to-rank bus turnaround; neither is
;              a write-path or power-down parameter and neither was in this
;              task's scope.
;   (the activate path was corrected in round 2, see the F4b block below)
;
; ============================================================================
; F4b (2026-09-21, round 2): ACTIVATE PATH ALSO BROUGHT TO JEDEC DDR5-4800
; ============================================================================
; Round 1 left tRRDR / tRRDW / RAW / tRAW at NVMain's DDR3-1333 template
; values. The rank-level stall counters in round 1's own stats show that these
; are not formalities: rrdWaits accounts for essentially every activate stall
; in all nine runs (it equals actWaits to within one event; 799,542 of them on
; lbm alone) and fawWaits is nonzero in eight of the nine. So they are
; corrected here rather than documented as harmless.
;
;   key        old -> new    new in ns   JEDEC source and arithmetic
;   tRRDR        5 -> 8        3.333 ns  JESD79-5 Table 521 (standard p.454):
;                                        tRRD_S = 8 nCK (ACTIVATE to ACTIVATE
;                                        for a DIFFERENT bank group), against
;                                        tRRD_L = max(8 nCK, 5 ns) = 12 nCK
;                                        for the SAME bank group
;                                        (5 / 0.4166667 = 12.0; max(8,12) = 12).
;                                        NVMain has one rank-wide activate-to-
;                                        activate value and no bank groups, so
;                                        the same argument as tWTR applies: a
;                                        16 Gb x8 part has 8 bank groups of 4
;                                        banks, only 1 activate pair in 8 is
;                                        same-group, and charging tRRD_L to all
;                                        eight groups would make DDR5 slower
;                                        than any real part in the direction
;                                        that flatters this project's own
;                                        conclusion. tRRD_S is therefore the
;                                        deliberate DDR5-friendly mapping.
;                                        Was 5 (2.083 ns), below even tRRD_S.
;   tRRDW        5 -> 8        3.333 ns  Same source. DEAD KEY: NVMain parses
;                                        tRRDW (Params.cpp:342) and stores it
;                                        (Params.h:153) but no timing code
;                                        anywhere reads it; tRRDR is used for
;                                        both directions. Changed only so the
;                                        file carries no DDR3 leftovers. It
;                                        cannot change any result.
;   RAW          4 -> 4        (count)   UNCHANGED and already right. RAW is
;                                        the NUMBER of activates in the rolling
;                                        window (StandardRank.cpp:113-120 sizes
;                                        the lastActivate[] ring with it), and
;                                        4 is literally JEDEC's four-activate
;                                        window.
;   tRAW        20 -> 32      13.333 ns  JESD79-5 Table 521 (standard p.454):
;                                        tFAW_1K = Max(32 nCK, 13.312 ns) for a
;                                        1 KB page. 13.312 / 0.4166667 = 31.949
;                                        -> 32 nCK, and max(32, 32) = 32.
;                                        1 KB is this part's page size: the
;                                        Micron 16 Gb DDR5 addendum [29]
;                                        Table 1 gives 1 KB for x4 and x8 and
;                                        2 KB only for x16, and DeviceWidth is
;                                        8 here. (tFAW_2K would be
;                                        Max(40 nCK, 16.640 ns) = 40 nCK; 1 KB
;                                        is both the correct one and the
;                                        DDR5-friendly one.) NVMain's window is
;                                        exactly JEDEC's: an ACTIVATE is
;                                        allowed only when
;                                        lastActivate[(RAWindex+1) % RAW]
;                                        + tRAW <= now, i.e. no more than RAW
;                                        activates in any tRAW window
;                                        (StandardRank.cpp:270, 616, 637).
;                                        Was 20 (8.333 ns).
;   tCCD         8 -> 8        3.333 ns  UNCHANGED and already right. JESD79-5
;                                        Table 521: tCCD_S = 8 nCK (CAS to CAS
;                                        for a different bank group, and the
;                                        same 8 nCK for BL16, BC8 fixed and
;                                        BC8 on-the-fly), against
;                                        tCCD_L = max(8 nCK, 5 ns) = 12 nCK for
;                                        the same bank group. NVMain again has
;                                        one rank-wide value, so the same
;                                        DDR5-friendly rule as tWTR and tRRDR
;                                        selects tCCD_S, which is what this
;                                        file already carried. Note that with
;                                        tBURST 8 the pacing term
;                                        MAX(tBURST, tCCD) is 8 either way, so
;                                        this key is not binding here.
;
; One NVMain-specific caveat, stated rather than papered over: NVMain charges
; tRRDR and advances the RAW ring on every REFRESH as well as on every ACTIVATE
; (StandardRank.cpp:602-604), i.e. it treats a refresh as an activate inside
; the four-activate window. JEDEC does not; DDR5 REFab is an all-bank command
; with its own tRFC. This is NVMain template behaviour with no DDR5
; counterpart, it is NOT configurable from this file, and it makes the model
; very slightly pessimistic for DDR5. It is recorded here so nobody reads the
; activate path as bit-exact JEDEC.
;
; PROVENANCE OF THE JEDEC NUMBERS IN THIS FILE: every JESD79-5 value above and
; in the F4 block was transcribed from the tables of JEDEC Standard No. 79-5
; (DDR5 SDRAM, July 2020) read from a third-party mirror,
; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RAMGuide/TheRamGuide-WIP-/main/DDR5%20Spec%20JESD79-5.pdf
; retrieved 2026-09-21, because the standard is access-gated at jedec.org and
; no copy exists under documents/. The table and page numbers are given for
; every value so each one can be re-verified line by line, and they SHOULD be
; re-verified against an official copy before publication.
; ============================================================================
;
; Memory configuration file examples
; As an example, the Micron DDR3-1333_4Gb_8B_x8 memory chip is defined

;================================================================================
; Interface specifications

; 666 MHz clock (1333 MT/s DDR). Clock period = 1.5 ns
CLK 2400

; Data Rate. 1 for SDR, 2 for DDR
RATE 2

; Bus width in bits. Two independent 32-bit subchannels (T2.4 correction;
; the plain live config wrongly modeled a single 64-bit-wide channel).
BusWidth 32

; Number of bits provided by each device in a rank
; Number of devices is calculated using BusWidth / DeviceWidth.
DeviceWidth 8

; NVMain use CLK 2400
; is actually used. So a simple CLK=1 and CPUFreq=4 still works for simulation. 
; However, it is straightforward to make it informative.
CPUFreq 3000
;================================================================================

;********************************************************************************
; General memory system configuration

; Number of banks per rank 
BANKS 32

; Number of ranks per channel
RANKS 1

; Number of channels in the system
CHANNELS 2

; Number of rows in one bank
ROWS 65536

; Number of VISIBLE columns in one LOGIC bank
COLS 64

; whether enable sub-array level parallelism (SALP)
; options: 
;   No SALP: MATHeight = ROWS
;   SALP: number of subarrays = ROWS / MATHeight
MATHeight 65536

; Whether use refresh? 
UseRefresh true

; the refresh granularity (the number of banks refreshed together in a rank)
; this must NOT be 0 when UseRefresh is true
; JEDEC recalibration (finding #7, 2026-07-11): DDR5 REFab is all-bank, so set
; to BANKS (32) for a single synchronized all-bank refresh event per rank
; rather than the inherited-from-template 8-of-32 grouping. Verified
; algebraically (and re-verified against the re-run) that this does NOT change
; total refresh energy/power -- SubArray's per-subarray charge
; (EIDD5B-EIDD3N)*tRFC/BANKS times BanksPerRefresh simultaneous subarray calls
; per event, times BANKS/BanksPerRefresh events per tREFI window, is
; (EIDD5B-EIDD3N)*tRFC regardless of BanksPerRefresh. This only changes
; concurrency realism (all banks blocked together during tRFC, matching REFab)
; and the per-bank refresh-event count/size split, not the module-sum total.
BanksPerRefresh 32

; RefreshRows is the number of rows to refresh per refresh operation
; for example, RefreshRows = ROWS/8192 for DRAM
; JEDEC recalibration: ROWS/8192 = 65536/8192 = 8, matching JEDEC's 8192 REF
; commands per tREFW window (was 4, inherited from the DDR3-1333 template).
RefreshRows 8

; the number of refresh that can be delayed
; options: 1 -- 8 (DDR-3 defines the maximum 9*tREFI)
; when 1 is applied, immediate refresh is used, otherwise the refresh can be
; delayed
DelayedRefreshThreshold 1; 
;********************************************************************************

;================================================================================
; Memory device timing parameters (in memory cycle) 

tBURST 8

tCMD 1 
tRAS 77
; Corrected 2026-08: tCAS/tRCD/tRP were 34-34-34 (unsourced/placeholder).
; Real DDR5-4800 standard (non-3DS) speed bin per SK hynix part-number decoder
; (documents/reference_validation_papers/TR-20230620152301091.pdf,
; TR-20230620152301412.pdf, code "EB" = "4800 40-39-39") is 40-39-39.
; [10] (JESD79-5D) itself remains access-gated; this is the best available
; grounding - see docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-29-book-reference-fixes.md.
tRCD 39
tAL 0
; F4b: CAS-to-CAS command delay, used by NVMain only as the burst pacing term
; MAX(tBURST, tCCD) (SubArray.cpp:417 and friends), rank-wide with no bank
; groups. JESD79-5 Table 521 (p.454): tCCD_S = 8 nCK for a different bank
; group (the same 8 nCK for BL16, BC8 fixed and BC8 on-the-fly), against
; tCCD_L = max(8 nCK, 5 ns) = 12 nCK for the same bank group. Same
; DDR5-friendly rule as tWTR and tRRDR selects tCCD_S = 8, which is what this
; file already carried, so this value is UNCHANGED. With tBURST 8 the pacing
; term is 8 either way, so tCCD is not binding in this configuration.
tCCD 8
; F4: CAS write latency. NVMain charges tCWD directly on the write request's
; own completion event (SubArray.cpp:671 writeEventTime = now + tCWD +
; MAX(tBURST,tCCD)*burstCount + writeTimer), so it IS the write latency, and
; it also sets the read-to-write turnaround (tCAS + tBURST + tRTRS - tCWD).
; With tAL 0 that is JEDEC WL = CWL exactly.
; JESD79-5 Table 471 (p.354): DDR5-4800B CWL = CL - 2 = 38 nCK = 15.833 ns at
; tCK 0.4166667. Was 7 (2.917 ns), the DDR3-1333 template's cycle count.
tCWD 38
; F4: write-to-read. NVMain measures it from the END of the write burst
; (nextRead = now + tCWD + tBURST + tWTR + writeTimer, SubArray.cpp:656),
; which is the JEDEC definition, but NVMain has a single tWTR, models no bank
; groups, and StandardRank.cpp:376 applies it to the WHOLE RANK. JEDEC splits
; it: tWTR_L = max(16 nCK, 10 ns) = 24 nCK for a read in the same bank group,
; tWTR_S = max(4 nCK, 2.5 ns) = 6 nCK for a different bank group
; (JESD79-5 Table 521, p.454). A 16 Gb x8 DDR5 device has 8 bank groups of 4
; banks, so with uniform bank use only 1 access in 8 pays tWTR_L. Mapping
; tWTR_L onto a rank-wide constraint would charge the same-bank-group penalty
; to all eight groups and make DDR5 slower than any real part, which would
; tilt the comparison toward this project's own conclusion. tWTR_S is
; therefore the conservative mapping: it is the common case and it gives the
; DDR5 baseline the benefit of the doubt. 2.5 / 0.4166667 = 6.0, max(4,6) = 6.
; Was 5 (2.083 ns). The tWTR_L bound, 24 nCK, is recorded in the F4 report as
; the pessimistic alternative.
; F4b: this choice is a deliberate DDR5-FRIENDLY JUDGEMENT, not a transcription,
; and it was reviewed and upheld in round 2. It is the smaller of the two JEDEC
; values, so it keeps the corrected baseline from over-shooting into a DDR5
; that is slower than any real part. The same rule is applied to tRRDR and
; tCCD below. Any document describing this config must say so.
tWTR 6
; F4: write recovery, charged AFTER the burst (nextPrecharge = now + tAL +
; tCWD + tBURST + writeTimer + tWR, SubArray.cpp:644), exactly JEDEC tWR.
; JESD79-5 Table 521 (p.454) DDR5-4800 column: tWR = 29.952 ns, which is the
; 30.000 ns nominal scaled and rounded down to 1 ps, i.e. exactly 72 x 0.416.
; 30 / 0.4166667 = 72.0 nCK = 30.000 ns. Was 10 (4.167 ns).
tWR 72
tRP 39
tCAS 40

tRTRS 1 ; for DDR-1, tRTRS can be 0
; F4: internal read to precharge (nextPrecharge = now + tAL + tBURST + tRTP
; - tCCD, SubArray.cpp:439; implicit precharge at now + tAL + tRTP for
; READ_PRECHARGE), exactly JEDEC tRTP.
; JESD79-5 Table 521 (p.454): tRTP = max(12 nCK, 7.5 ns).
; 7.5 / 0.4166667 = 18.0, max(12, 18) = 18 nCK = 7.500 ns. Was 5 (2.083 ns).
tRTP 18
; JEDEC recalibration (finding #7): tRFC1 @ 16Gb = 295ns * CLK(2400MHz) = 708
; cycles. Old value (107 cycles = 44.58ns @ 2400MHz) reverse-solves to 160.5ns
; at the DDR3-1333 template's original 666.667MHz/1.5ns clock -- inherited,
; never rescaled when CLK was overridden to 2400 for this DDR5 config.
tRFC 708
tOST 1 ; ODT switching time

; F4b: the ONE activate-to-activate constraint NVMain applies. Set on every
; ACTIVATE (StandardRank.cpp:283) and on every REFRESH (:602), rank-wide, with
; no bank groups; the rrdWaits counter (:658) measures it and it is the binding
; activate stall in all nine DDR5 runs.
; JESD79-5 Table 521 (p.454): tRRD_S = 8 nCK (different bank group);
; tRRD_L = max(8 nCK, 5 ns) = 12 nCK (same bank group, 5 / 0.4166667 = 12.0).
; Deliberate DDR5-friendly mapping to tRRD_S, same argument as tWTR: only 1
; activate pair in 8 is same-bank-group on an 8-bank-group part, so charging
; tRRD_L rank-wide would slow DDR5 beyond any real part in the direction of
; this project's own conclusion. 8 nCK = 3.333 ns. Was 5 (2.083 ns).
tRRDR 8
; F4b: DEAD KEY. Parsed at Params.cpp:342, stored at Params.h:153, read by no
; timing code anywhere in NVMain (whole-tree grep); tRRDR is used for both
; directions, as the stock comment above said. Set to tRRD_S for consistency
; only; it cannot change any result.
tRRDW 8

; Row Activation Window (RAW), which specifies the maximum Activation in a
; rolling window. tRAW is the corresponding window width (in cycle).
; Options:
;   for 2D DRAM, RAW = 4, Four Activation Window (FAW)
;   for WideI/O DRAM, RAW = 2, Two Activation Window (TAW)
; F4b: RAW 4 is already JEDEC's four-activate window and is unchanged.
; StandardRank.cpp:113-120 uses it to size the lastActivate[] ring, and the
; gate at :270 admits an ACTIVATE only when
; lastActivate[(RAWindex+1) % RAW] + tRAW <= now, i.e. at most RAW activates in
; any tRAW window. That is exactly tFAW.
RAW 4
; F4b: JESD79-5 Table 521 (p.454): tFAW_1K = Max(32 nCK, 13.312 ns).
; 13.312 / 0.4166667 = 31.949 -> 32 nCK; max(32, 32) = 32 nCK = 13.333 ns.
; 1 KB is this part's page size (Micron 16 Gb DDR5 addendum [29] Table 1:
; 1 KB for x4 and x8, 2 KB only for x16; DeviceWidth is 8 here). tFAW_2K would
; be Max(40 nCK, 16.640 ns) = 40 nCK, so 1 KB is both the correct choice and
; the DDR5-friendly one. The fawWaits counter (:665) is nonzero in eight of the
; nine DDR5 runs, so this constraint does bind. Was 20 (8.333 ns).
tRAW 32

; F4 review caveat (2026-09-21): the tRDPDEN / tWRPDEN / tWRAPDEN formulas below (JESD79-5 Table 272 as
; transcribed via a mirror) were not independently corroborated and may be off by one cycle (0.417 ns).
; They gate power-down ENTRY only, tWRAPDEN is never read by NVMain, and no reported result depends on
; them at that precision. Re-verify against an official copy of the standard before citing the table.
; powerdown mode enter and exit
; F4: all four power-down parameters below come from JESD79-5 Table 272
; ("Power Down Timing Parameters", standard p.144). PowerDownMode is FASTEXIT
; and UseLowPower is true, so the rank really does enter and leave power-down
; during a run and these values are live, not decoration.
;
; Earliest power-down entry after a READ without auto-precharge
; (DDR3Bank.cpp:400-402 nextPowerDown = now + burst offset + tRDPDEN).
; JEDEC: tRDPDEN = RL + RBL/2 + 1 nCK. RL = tAL + tCAS = 0 + 40; BL16 on this
; 32-bit subchannel is tBURST 8 nCK so RBL/2 = 8. 40 + 8 + 1 = 49 nCK
; = 20.417 ns. Was 24.
tRDPDEN 49
; Earliest power-down entry after a WRITE without auto-precharge
; (DDR3Bank.cpp:487-489). JEDEC: tWRPDEN = WL + WBL/2 + ceil(tWR/tCK) + 1
; (Table 272 note 2 requires rounding tWR/tCK up) = 38 + 8 + 72 + 1 = 119 nCK
; = 49.583 ns. Was 19.
tWRPDEN 119
; Same after a WRITE with auto-precharge. JEDEC: tWRAPDEN = WL + WBL/2 + WR + 1
; with WR the MR6-programmed write recovery in clock cycles = 72, so
; 38 + 8 + 72 + 1 = 119 nCK = 49.583 ns. Was 22. NVMain parses this key
; (Params.cpp:356) but no timing code anywhere reads it, so this value is
; inert; it is corrected only so the file carries no DDR3 leftovers.
tWRAPDEN 119
; Minimum time spent in power-down (StandardRank.cpp:495 and DDR3Bank.cpp:229
; both set the power-up-allowed point to now + tPD).
; JEDEC Table 272: tPD = max(7.5 ns, 8 nCK). 7.5 / 0.4166667 = 18.0,
; max(18, 8) = 18 nCK = 7.500 ns. Was 6 (2.500 ns).
tPD 18
; Power-down exit to next valid command (StandardRank.cpp:545,549 puTimer;
; DDR3Bank.cpp:275-290 pushes every next* to now + tXP). Reached for both PDA
; and PDPF, which is what FASTEXIT produces.
; JEDEC Table 272: tXP = max(7.5 ns, 8 nCK) = 18 nCK = 7.500 ns.
; Was 6 (2.500 ns).
tXP 18
; NOT corrected. DDR5 has no DLL-off slow-exit power-down (JESD79-5 Table 271:
; DLL On, Fast exit, for active and precharged alike), so no JEDEC parameter
; maps onto tXPDLL, and NVMain only reads it in the STANDARDRANK_PDPS branch,
; which PowerDownMode FASTEXIT never reaches. Left at the template value
; rather than invented.
tXPDLL 17
; NOT corrected. Self-refresh exit. NVMain never enters self refresh; the only
; reader of tXS/tXSDLL is the DRAMPower2 trace writer, whose AddHook PostTrace
; line is commented out at the bottom of this file. Inert.
tXS 5
tXSDLL 512

; refresh window between two refreshes to a cell (in cycle, e.g., 64ms/tCK)
; options: for DDR3, 64ms (normal) or 32ms (thermal extended)
;          for LPDDR3, 32ms (normal) or 16ms (thermal extended)
;          for 3D DRAM, 32ms/16ms/8ms are all possible
; JEDEC recalibration (finding #7, 2026-07-11): DDR5 tREFW = 32ms * CLK(2400MHz)
; = 76,800,000 cycles. Old value (42,666,667 cycles) reverse-solves to exactly
; 64ms at 666.667MHz/1.5ns -- the DDR3-1333 template's clock, carried over
; unrescaled when CLK was overridden to 2400 for this DDR5 config (see the
; stale "666 MHz clock... Clock period = 1.5ns" comment at the top of this
; file). NVMain's "current" EnergyModel (StandardRank.cpp:977-995) computes
; simulationTime as raw elapsed cycles with no separate CLK-to-ns conversion,
; so cycles here must equal real_seconds * CLK directly -- confirmed no hidden
; internal clock domain applies.
tREFW 76800000
;================================================================================

;********************************************************************************
; Memory device energy and power parameters
 
; Read/write values are in nano Joules
; NOTES:
;   NVSIM energy is per word
;   Erd is the read energy from a single mat
;   Ewr is the write energy (SET or RESET, they are the same)
;   These values are the energys required to read a page into a row buffer.
;
;   Other energy values are taken from CACTI
;

; whether use low power mode?
UseLowPower true

; PowerDown mode 
; Option:
;    FASTEXIT: Precharge PowerDown with Fast Exit
;    SLOWEXIT: Precharge PowerDown with Slow Exit
;    <TO DO>: extend to support more power down mode 
PowerDownMode FASTEXIT

EnergyModel current

; Subarray write energy per bit
Ewrpb 0.000202

Erd 3.405401
Eopenrd 1.081080
Ewr 1.023750
Eref 38.558533
Eactstdby 0.090090
Eprestdby 0.083333
Epda 0.000000
Epdpf 0.078829
Epdps 0.000000

; DRAM style power calculation. All values below in mA, taken from datasheet.

; JEDEC recalibration (finding #8a, 2026-07-11): DDR5 VDD/VDDQ = 1.1V per
; JESD79-5 (was 1.5V, the DDR3/DDR4-era default -- also literally NVMain's
; stock Params.cpp class default, never customized for this config at all).
Voltage 1.1

; Finding #8b (cycle 6c, 2026-07-12): EIDD0-EIDD5B current magnitudes sourced
; from SK hynix 16Gb DDR5 datasheet, x8 @ DDR5-4800 ("hynix floor" -- the
; headline config, persisted here; a Micron-ceiling variant was run in
; parallel as a band, see results/system_v2_3B/ and the cycle-6c report at
; results/cycle6c_ddr5_calibration_and_provenance_report.md). hynix does not
; publish IDD2P/IDD3P: EIDD2P0/EIDD2P1 = Micron IDD2P(88) x hynix:Micron
; IDD2N ratio (49/92) = 46.87; EIDD3P = Micron IDD3P(140) x hynix:Micron
; IDD3N ratio (117.6/142) = 115.94 -- documented interpolation, not measured.
; EIDD1/EIDD2NT/EIDD6 left at stock placeholders: confirmed dead (EIDD1/EIDD6
; parsed but never read by any energy formula; EIDD2NT not even declared in
; Params.h/parsed by Params.cpp -- see cycle-6 EIDD audit), so their value has
; zero effect on any simulated result. EIDD6 (T4-10) remains an unsourced
; 12 mA placeholder here specifically because no SK hynix chip-level IDD6
; datasheet value could be confirmed with confidence (/research pass,
; 2026-09-05, documents/MBMM_Book_Typst/research_notes/item4_ddr5_typical_current.md)
; -- the counterpart Micron-ceiling config (DDR5_4800_DRAM_micron.config) was
; updated to a real, sourced value (Micron [29] Table 6, IDD6N=102mA) since
; that datasheet was findable; no equivalent hynix figure was available to
; interpolate from the way EIDD2P0/EIDD3P above already are.
EIDD0 60.2
EIDD1 54
EIDD2P0 46.87
EIDD2P1 46.87
EIDD2N 49
EIDD2NT 37
EIDD3P 115.94
EIDD3N 117.6
EIDD4R 259
EIDD4W 288
EIDD5B 227
EIDD6 12
;********************************************************************************

;================================================================================
; Memory controller parameters

; Specify which memory controller to use
; options: PerfectMemory, FCFS, FRFCFS, FRFCFS-WQF, DRC (for 3D DRAM Cache)
MEM_CTL FRFCFS

; whether dump the memory request trace?
CTL_DUMP false

; whether use close-page row buffer management policy?
; options: 
;   0--Open-Page, the row will be closed until a row buffer miss occurs
;   1--Relaxed Close-Page, the row will be closed if no other row buffer hit exists
;   2--Restricted Close-Page, the row will be closed immediately, no row
;      buffer hit can be exploited
ClosePage 0

; command scheduling scheme
; options: 0--fixed priority, 1--rank first round-robin, 2--bank first round-robin
ScheduleScheme 2

; address mapping scheme
; options: SA:R:RK:BK:CH:C (SA-Subarray, R-row, C:column, BK:bank, RK:rank, CH:channel)
AddressMappingScheme SA:R:RK:BK:CH:C

; interconnect between controller and memory chips
; options: OffChipBus (for 2D), OnChipBus (for 3D)
INTERCONNECT OffChipBus

; FRFCFS-WQF specific parameters
ReadQueueSize 32 ; read queue size

WriteQueueSize 32 ; write queue size

HighWaterMark 32 ; write drain high watermark. write drain is triggerred if it is reached

LowWaterMark 16 ; write drain low watermark. write drain is stopped if it is reached
;================================================================================

;********************************************************************************
; Simulation control parameters
;
PrintGraphs false
PrintPreTrace false
PreTraceFile mcf.trace
EchoPreTrace false
PeriodicStatsInterval 100000000

TraceReader NVMainTrace
;********************************************************************************

;================================================================================
; Endurance model parameters
; This is used for Non-volatile memory

EnduranceModel NullModel
EnduranceDist Normal
EnduranceDistMean 1000000 
EnduranceDistVariance  100000
FlipNWriteGranularity 32

; Everything below this can be overridden for heterogeneous channels
;CONFIG_CHANNEL0 lp_rram.config
;CONFIG_CHANNEL1 hp_rram.config

; whether set the DRAM in powerdown mode at the beginning?
InitPD false

IgnoreData true
;================================================================================

;AddHook PostTrace
;PostTraceFile drampower2.trace
;PostTraceWriter DRAMPower2Trace
;DRAMPower2XML nvmain_config.xml 

